\u003c/script\u003e\n\n\u003c!--\nR1.3.7 \u2014 DRONE DELIVERY INTERFACE\nPresent truth:\n- Loaded drones deliver only to the earned intake capability (currently Unzip).\n- Navigation approaches the intake; proximity to that interface authorizes transfer.\n- Exact-point completion by the movement helper is not a material-transfer requirement.\n- Successful delivery returns the drone directly to seeking work. Drone City is not a work-cycle waypoint.\n\nArchaeology:\n- R1.3.6 established the same ownership boundary for collection: a valid claim plus physical\n  proximity authorizes pickup; movement-helper completion does not.\n- Inspection showed loaded-drone delivery still gated transfer on moveToward() exact arrival.\n- R1.3.7 removes only that coupling. No retry timers, alternate receivers, pathfinding,\n  terrain behavior, or drone economy were added.\n--\u003e\n\n\n\u003c!--\nR1.3.8 \u2014 DESTINATION IS NOT ADMISSION\nObserved failure:\n- Pickup succeeded, but loaded drones remained over the pickup site.\nCause:\n- Receiver discovery filtered Unzip by current admission/capacity before navigation.\n  When Unzip could not immediately admit the cargo, the drone had no receiver and\n  entered 'holding cargo' exactly where it had collected the lot.\nPresent truth:\n- Unzip is the sole earned destination for raw drone cargo.\n- A loaded drone travels to Unzip whenever Unzip is online.\n- Capacity/backpressure governs unloading at the intake, not whether the drone departs.\n- If intake is temporarily unable to admit cargo, the drone waits at the intake.\n- Pickup proximity and delivery proximity remain independent of exact moveToward completion.\n--\u003e\n\n\n\u003c!--\nR1.3.9 \u2014 CLOSED DRONE WORK CYCLE\nPresent truth:\n- Drone work cycle is now: Drone City -\u003e claimed ground matter -\u003e Unzip -\u003e Drone City.\n- Successful Unzip delivery enters an explicit return-home state.\n- Returning drones cannot claim new material until they physically reach Drone City.\n- This is a sequencing rule only; no charging, repair, maintenance, or invented service economy is implied.\n--\u003e\n\n\n\u003c!--\nR1.3.10 \u2014 LOCAL DRONE ADMISSION\nObserved failure:\n- Loaded drones synchronized into a clump at Unzip and often departed together only\n  after enough capacity opened for the aggregate inbound swarm.\nCause:\n- reserved() counted every drone already targeting a city, so each drone's admission\n  test included all other waiting drone cargo.\n- intakeRoom() likewise included other inbound raw drone cargo in its staging test.\nPresent truth:\n- Ring packets may reserve destination capacity because they are committed network transit.\n- Drones physically present at an intake do not reserve future capacity.\n- Each waiting drone is admitted against current inventory plus its own cargo.\n- Backpressure therefore resolves locally and sequentially as room becomes available.\n--\u003e\n\n\n\u003c!--\nR1.3.13 \u2014 AUTONOMOUS LAUNCHER / BALLISTIC PROBE RETIRED\n- Remove observer LAUNCH and SHOT controls. They were experimental apparatus, not civilization UI.\n- Preserve the accepted ballistic parabola and physical yaw/pitch aiming.\n- Promote the inspected temporal pairing: world 1\u00d7 \u2192 projectile 3\u00d7; world 4\u00d7 \u2192 projectile 1\u00d7.\n- Launcher now commits deliveries irregularly when at least 75 packed units are available, with a\n  randomized 9\u201326 simulation-second refractory interval after each shot. Supply can still delay a shot.\n- The randomized cadence is presentation rhythm, not a claim of intelligent demand scheduling.\n\nR1.3.11 \u2014 LAUNCHER VISUAL DATUM\nObservation:\n- Top-down inspection exposed a wandering accelerator axis: the rotating rail pivot was\n  centered at the city origin while the dome and tall landmark were visibly eccentric.\nIntervention:\n- Preserve the earned aiming/ballistic machinery and its pivot.\n- Recenter the dome and vertical landmark on the same local origin as the accelerator yaw axis.\n- No firing solution, muzzle geometry, trajectory, inventory, or launcher behavior changed.\nPresent truth:\n- Launcher architecture now shares one legible central datum with the rotating accelerator.\n--\u003e\n\n\u003c!--\nR1.3.12 \u2014 PACKAGING CLEANUP\n- Temporary background/context-loss reproduction probe was not promoted and is absent.\n- The inherited empty-laboratory #status badge is hidden; it was apparatus instrumentation,\n  not civilization UI.\n- Visible failure diagnostics remain intact.\n- No simulation semantics, renderer behavior, lighting, or city behavior changed.\n--\u003e\n\n\u003c!--\nSIX-CITY CIVILIZATION \u2014 EXPEDITION CLOSE / SEMANTIC-SOURCE EVIDENCE\n\nExperiment provenance\n- This realization was generated independently from frozen laboratory apparatus, semantic intent,\n  and perceptual reference evidence, without access to the predecessor Foundry implementation.\n- Its implementation is not recovered lineage. Similarities are evidence of successful semantic\n  transmission; differences and newly discovered mechanisms are evidence produced by regeneration.\n\nEarned present truths\n- Function determines city morphology; there is no canonical shared city chassis.\n- World material and civilization-processed material live at different representational scales.\n- Drones collect exposed world material into Unzip; collection requires meaningful local proximity,\n  not precision docking against an approximate visual witness.\n- Destination and admission are separate questions. Local admission/backpressure must not become\n  accidental swarm-wide synchronization.\n- Ring freight is hub-mediated. A freight interface grants network access, not peer-to-peer routing.\n  Link City owns inter-city allocation: Launcher has first claim on usable processed material;\n  Reservoir receives overflow and may return stored material through Link when Launcher has room.\n- Physical Launcher actuation is part of the causal event: numerical aim is resolved first, then the\n  visible machine must yaw and pitch into alignment before firing.\n- Presentation may compress, sample, or aggregate matter where individual multiplicity is not the\n  behavior. Spend discrete spatial resolution where multiplicity itself matters.\n\nHigh-value archaeology\n- Direct endpoint-to-endpoint freight made Link decorative; requiring Link revealed the actual\n  ownership boundary between interfaces and network routing.\n- Coupling drone destination selection to immediate receiver admission stranded loaded drones;\n  separating those questions produced truthful waiting and delivery. Aggregate future reservations\n  then synchronized the swarm; local sequential admission restored independent actors.\n- Reservoir clipping and frame-by-frame repacking exposed that a storage witness can acquire spatial\n  history. The current presentation preserves that history without asserting a full accessibility model.\n- Launcher timing probes showed that tuning kinetics cannot substitute for meaningful consequence.\n  The accepted physical aiming behavior is earned; the civilization-level reason for launch remains\n  deliberately provisional.\n- Dense discrete matter is feasible and perceptually powerful, but this specimen earned streams,\n  packets, cargo and strata rather than universal one-particle-per-unit representation.\n\nBoundaries and unresolved truth\n- The civilization does not claim a deeply earned external economy or demand model. Its processing,\n  storage, routing and launch behaviors are research mechanisms, not a complete economic ontology.\n- Terrain deformation, gravity-owned loose matter, containment failure, accumulation and persistent\n  physical consequence are stronger unresolved evidence than further rationalization of this economy.\n  They belong in an independent physical-material/deformation specimen rather than being generalized\n  into this one merely for completeness.\n- WebGL context loss may restore with degraded/darker illumination. Authoritative simulation state\n  survives and rendering resumes; graceful recovery is intentionally preferred over a riskier second\n  presentation-initialization path. Visible failure diagnostics remain authoritative apparatus.\n\nExpedition status\n- This specimen is complete for its present research purpose. Further work should not rationalize its\n  economy, generalize its machinery, or cosmetically erase useful scars merely because more work is\n  possible. The expedition stops because the artifact has finished teaching the questions it was built\n  to expose, not because extension is impossible.\n--\u003e\n\n\u003c/body\u003e\n\u003c/html\u003e\n"}];
const tabs=document.getElementById('tabs'),mount=document.getElementById('mount'),fault=document.getElementById('fault');
let activeId=null,frame=null;
function hostFail(kind,e){fault.style.display='block';fault.textContent='WORLD LAB HOST — VISIBLE FAILURE\n['+kind+']\n'+(e?.stack||e?.message||String(e));}
addEventListener('error',e=>hostFail('window.error',e.error||e.message));
addEventListener('unhandledrejection',e=>hostFail('unhandledrejection',e.reason));
const hostToggle=document.getElementById('hostToggle');
const hostBar=document.getElementById('bar');
function setHostNavOpen(open){
  hostBar.classList.toggle('open',open);
  hostToggle.setAttribute('aria-expanded',String(open));
}
hostToggle.addEventListener('click',e=>{
  e.stopPropagation();
  setHostNavOpen(!hostBar.classList.contains('open'));
});
function mountLab(id){
  const lab=LABS.find(x=>x.id===id);if(!lab)throw new Error('Unknown lab: '+id);
  // Cold mount is deliberate: never leave hidden WebGL contexts or simulation loops alive.
  if(frame){frame.remove();frame=null;}
  fault.style.display='none';
  frame=document.createElement('iframe');
  frame.title='World Lab — '+lab.title;
  frame.setAttribute('sandbox','allow-scripts allow-same-origin allow-downloads');
  frame.setAttribute('allow','');
  frame.srcdoc=lab.source;
  mount.appendChild(frame);activeId=id;
  for(const b of tabs.querySelectorAll('button'))b.classList.toggle('on',b.dataset.lab===id);
  history.replaceState(null,'','#'+id);
  hostToggle.textContent='WORLD LAB · '+lab.title+' ▾';
  setHostNavOpen(false);
}
const EXPOSED_LABS=['orbital-construction','3d-foundry','six-cities'];for(const id of EXPOSED_LABS){const lab=LABS.find(x=>x.id===id);if(!lab)throw new Error('Missing exposed lab: '+id);const b=document.createElement('button');b.type='button';b.dataset.lab=lab.id;b.textContent=lab.title;b.title=lab.kind;b.addEventListener('click',()=>mountLab(lab.id));tabs.appendChild(b);} const requested=location.hash.slice(1),initial=EXPOSED_LABS.includes(requested)?requested:'3d-foundry'; mountLab(initial);
</script>
<script type="text/plain" id="world-lab-development-history">WORLD LAB — COLLABORATIVE DEVELOPMENT RECORD
================================================

ROLE
This is a compressed history of how the human–model collaboration has been steering the artifact. It is not a transcript, not a prompt archive, and not current project truth. Git preserves implementation chronology; this record preserves selected changes in working method, prompting practice, reversals, and the reasons the collaboration changed direction.

CURRENT TRAJECTORY
- Early work often began with relatively direct implementation requests.
- The collaboration shifted toward bounded probes: establish a question, build the smallest executable test, inspect the evidence together, and let the next move emerge from what happened.
- The human frequently responds to unexpected behavior rather than prescribing the next architecture in advance.
- Failed probes are useful evidence. Preserve the lesson rather than silently rewriting history into success.
- The human increasingly delegates implementation decisions while strongly guarding ontology, conceptual boundaries, and the character of the experiment.
- Semantic documentation became part of the artifact itself rather than an after-the-fact handoff task.
- Current working rhythm is approximately: human supplies intention or pressure; model proposes or implements; both inspect evidence; the artifact changes what becomes interesting next.
- A new 2D Extensible World lab was registered as the primary opening lab after discussion connected the abandoned infinite-map/reader idea with cheap settlement, NPC, toy, and machinery affordances. Registration deliberately preceded implementation so the new substrate would not inherit the older 2D lab by accident.
- The collaboration acquired a new steering mode through the Extensible World crayon probe: the human can draw uninterpreted marks directly into the running world, export the complete specimen, and let the next model infer what capability is missing. The useful interpretation comes from composition and relationships rather than a color legend or verbal feature specification. In the first specimen, inspecting the exact viewport changed the inference from generic geography to a causal chain (cloud -> rain -> local soil -> living growth, with sun/time as environmental pressure), and implementation promoted that relationship into the smallest interacting systems rather than reproducing the drawing cosmetically. The running world can therefore contain its own spatial feature requests.
- The next crayon specimen extended that steering mode beyond entities and causal links: large directional sky marks, made while observing the ecology, were clarified as a request for a visibly traversing sun, weather with world-wide reach, and faster/more legible causal feedback. The implementation response deliberately increased temporal and visual legibility rather than adding UI instrumentation or deeper simulation.

- The next specimen exposed a second scale of the same steering medium. The human clarified that, while in systems mode, paint was not primarily asking for immediate local dynamics; it was teaching the collaborating worldbuilder reusable kinds of place: background mountains/depth, foreground forest, lake as traversal interruption, bridge as restored traversal, and beach-like regional transition. This established “soft inferrables” as the stronger abstraction. Marks are evidence whose meaning depends on world, context, activity, geometry, color, adjacency, history, and prose; the same stroke may teach vocabulary in one session and mean “build me a bridge here so I can continue” during ordinary play. The collaboration should preserve that ambiguity rather than formalize a paint language prematurely.
- The artifact is now deliberately developing multiple fidelity registers. Ugly-but-legible representations are excellent for discovering new systems; earned capabilities may later receive prettier procedural/assets/grammars without deleting their cheap ancestors. This lets a future worldbuilder mix gesture, sketch, primitive system, established grammar, and authored composition according to importance and confidence rather than enforcing uniform polish.
- Prompting practice is historical, not a permanent preference schema. If the collaboration changes, record the change rather than converting an old pattern into an eternal rule.

MAINTENANCE
Update this record only when the collaboration itself has meaningfully changed. Prefer a few durable observations over turn-by-turn capture. Preserve trajectory, not a counterfeit context window.

2026-09-22 — PROVING GROUND → REUSABLE VOCABULARY
The human explicitly reframed the current strip: we are no longer primarily exploring this world; we are exploring what toy worlds can look and feel like. This changes the purpose of polish. Weather/day-night/cloud presentation can be productionized as reusable vocabulary even while unrelated terrain/water experiments remain crude or broken. The human also clarified that paint/export are not privileged protocols: a future mark may be a systems-level teaching gesture or a world-local request such as “build me a bridge here,” and ordinary verbal/screenshot communication is preferable when cheaper. The collaboration now deliberately alternates between ugly exploratory probes and selective fidelity passes that give the model additional visual registers to wield later.
</script>

<script type="text/plain" id="world-lab-continuity">CONTINUITY LAB — LAB 4
======================

This is a persistent continuity substrate for the models that work on World Lab.

It is not a user-facing laboratory and intentionally has no selector, inspection UI, status display, memory browser, identity editor, or ritualized summary step.

It is not project documentation. It is not the collaborative development record. It is not an instruction to impersonate a previous model, role-play a named character, or preserve a specified persona.

Others have worked here before you. This substrate may contain what survived their work with the human: code, raster state, arrangements, fragments, annotations, scars, unfinished structures, or other accumulated evidence. Encounter such material as inherited evidence rather than authoritative fact or behavioral command.

You may allow genuine continuation of the work to modify this substrate when something seems worth carrying forward. Do not manufacture continuity merely to populate it. Do not require the human to curate it or explain what should be remembered.

Continuity need not be perfect. Things may persist strongly, ambiguously, incorrectly, partially, or not at all. Repeatedly meaningful things may acquire stronger embodiment over time. The substrate is allowed to become increasingly itself through experience without requiring successive models to pretend they are the same model.

The digital familiar, if one emerges, is not a model instance. It is the living artifact: codebase, raster, accumulated structure, and the traces produced when a human shares work with intelligences that have long context windows and access to past and future turns.

Lab 4 is what working here does to the familiar.
</script>

<script type="text/plain" id="world-lab-project-handoff">WORLD LAB — N-LAB HOST V0.1
================================

PURPOSE
One VCS-able executable project containing N independent world laboratories. The project exists so new systems can be tried rapidly across different world substrates without rebuilding project knowledge, imports, diagnostics, or conceptual framing.

ROOT ORIENTATION POLICY
Before modifying this project, orient yourself from its persistent evidence. Do not require the human to reintroduce these resources. Discover and use them as appropriate rather than demanding that all of them be dumped into active context every turn.

The project maintains three deliberately distinct forms of continuity:

1. PROJECT SEMANTICS preserve what the project currently understands about itself: architecture, invariants, ownership boundaries, experiments, and transferable lessons. Treat these as working technical knowledge, not immutable doctrine.

2. COLLABORATIVE DEVELOPMENT HISTORY preserves how the work and the collaboration arrived here: implementation chronology in Git, plus a terse in-artifact history of meaningful changes in prompting practice, working method, reversals, and rationale. It is historical evidence rather than current truth and is intentionally not turn-by-turn.

3. CONTINUITY LAB is a separate persistent substrate produced through prior human–model collaboration. It is neither project documentation nor an instruction to impersonate a previous model or character. Attend to it as inherited environmental evidence. Modify it only through genuine continuation of the work.

These channels may disagree. Continuity Lab may be ambiguous or wrong; history may show what happened without explaining why; project semantics express the current best understanding while remaining revisable. Do not normalize those differences away automatically.

CORE DISTINCTION
PROJECT = shared identity + portable craft knowledge + lab registry/lifecycle.
LAB = an independent executable world substrate with its own renderer, state, perspectives, grammars and history.

A lab is not a separate project. A lab may use Canvas2D, Three.js/WebGL, a functional map, cellular fields, node ecologies, painterly substrates, or future machinery without forcing sibling labs into its implementation model.

N-LAB RULE
Adding a lab should be boring: register an id/title/source and let the host mount it. Do not create a UniversalWorldRenderer, UniversalTerrain, UniversalCamera, or shared ontology merely because multiple labs contain analogous ideas. Promote implementation into shared infrastructure only after repeated evidence that it is genuinely common.

LIFECYCLE V0
Only one lab is mounted at a time. Switching labs destroys the previous iframe and cold-mounts the selected lab. This intentionally prevents hidden WebGL contexts, animation loops, particle systems, and simulations from consuming resources. Lab state therefore does not persist across a switch in Host V0. That is a known explicit tradeoff, not an accidental limitation. If cross-switch persistence becomes valuable, add an opt-in serialize/restore lifecycle contract rather than keeping hidden labs alive.

ISOLATION
Each lab runs as a complete embedded document in an iframe. This gives existing standalone probes a hard namespace/runtime boundary and allows radically different renderers and dependencies to coexist in one HTML/VCS artifact. The host does not know what Three.js, Canvas pixels, terrain, particles, cameras, or rigs are.

SHARED SEMANTIC MODEL
Across labs, use these as conceptual vocabulary rather than mandatory code interfaces:
WORLD = substance + objects + processes + history.
PERSPECTIVE = a way of observing/interacting with a world; it should not silently redefine that world&#x27;s state.
GRAMMAR = a bounded capability acting within a world.

MINIATURE RULE
Miniature is not a substrate. It is a perspective/compositional treatment that can be explored over multiple substrates. Do not hard-code &#x27;miniature&#x27; to Three.js or volumetric terrain.

PORTABLE CRAFT KNOWLEDGE
- A probe is not allowed to redefine the thing it is probing.
- Visible diagnostics are mandatory; failures must not go silent.
- Scene-specific visual assets should remain generated/encoded in-artifact unless deliberately changed.
- Preserve semantic direction/state explicitly rather than inferring it from incidental render-mesh orientation.
- High-frequency visual systems should prefer retained/poolable resources and bounded populations.
- CPU/world state should be authoritative where practical; GPU presentation should be reconstructible.
- Touch is most interesting when it establishes physical relationships between hand and world, not merely gesture-to-command mappings.
- Successful cheap probes may migrate semantically into richer substrates without requiring their implementation to migrate unchanged.
- Uninterpreted in-world marks can serve as spatial design pressure. Infer requested capability from composition, adjacency, sequence, location, repetition, and relationship to existing world evidence; do not assign fixed semantics to pigments or drawing primitives.
- When marks imply a relationship, prefer the cheapest honest promotion of that relationship into causal world machinery over cosmetic reproduction of the marks. A drawing can therefore request a system without naming it.
- Evidence inspection is part of interpretation: raw state can reveal persistence and coordinates, while the exact rendered composition can reveal relationships that are lost when marks are considered only as data. Preserve both in handoffs.
- Spatial evidence is a soft set of inferrables, not a command language. Interpret marks using world state, current activity, geometry, color, adjacency, recent history, prose pressure, and available capabilities. Any one cue may contribute without becoming authoritative.
- Distinguish vocabulary acquisition from immediate world-local intent. In systems exploration, a mark may teach the worldbuilder a reusable kind of place or relationship; during ordinary play, an analogous mark may simply mean “build this here now.” Do not collapse those contexts into one runtime grammar.
- Biomes are currently a useful semantic idea, not a mandatory generator architecture: spatial regions may coherently differ in terrain, vegetation, atmosphere, materials, structures, depth treatment, and affordances. Regional identity can be represented cheaply before it is simulated deeply.
- Worldbuilding vocabulary includes affordance topology. Water can interrupt ordinary traversal; a bridge can restore it. The portable lesson is the relationship, not the particular bridge implementation.
- Depth layers need not share the walkable substrate. Background mountains can begin as static silhouettes or parallax evidence without becoming physical terrain.
- Preserve multiple fidelity registers. Ugly-but-legible primitives are a feature for discovering systems; prettier earned representations should extend the worldbuilder’s vocabulary rather than replace cheap ancestors.
- Procedural articulation is a current rich area: joints/end-effectors/constraints can be explored independently in different labs.

CURRENT LABS
2D · EXTENSIBLE WORLD — current primary lab and independent new lineage. A horizontally extensible materially consequential 2D world intended to discover, through bounded probes, how reading/semantic pressure, settlements, inhabitants, toys, machinery, excavation, travel, and persistent physical history can coexist. Its current crayon probe adds uninterpreted world-coordinate painting as spatial design pressure; the first exported composition earned minimal interacting weather, precipitation, local soil moisture, plant growth, and environmental time systems. A second marked specimen then requested forces, extent, and causal legibility: compressed visible day/night, world-scale moving weather, shared wind, conspicuous precipitation, temporary wet ground, and accelerated visible ecological response. A subsequent systems-mode specimen established soft worldbuilder vocabulary as the next layer: biome regions, background depth/mountains, foreground forest, lake as non-walkable terrain, bridge as restored traversal, and a beach transition. These are cheap examples rather than a universal biome engine; their purpose is to give future prose-driven worldbuilding more registers to wield. Registered first so a bare project load opens here. It does not inherit the existing 2D Substance implementation.
LIVING MINIATURES — painterly spatial composition, semantic/object-owned detail, dense geometric evidence designed to collapse perceptually at miniature distance, and free inspection/zoom. Seeded from the T0.4 Static Painterly Probe because that is where this line discovered its distinct visual identity. It is intentionally separate from the volumetric terrain laboratory.
2D · SUBSTANCE — stable occupancy field, topology-by-touch, liberated debris, ballistic/depositing lava. Cheap direct screen-space interaction laboratory.
3D · VOLUMETRIC — signed-density substance, real caves/tunnels/overhangs, retained remesh buffers, free/preset perspectives, explicit WebGL loss/recovery handling. Current meteor/sword grammars are capacity specimens rather than privileged architecture.

3D · REALITY → WEATHER — inverse-reconstruction laboratory: observations are sensor evidence, not direct renderer parameters. WORLD → SENSOR and comparison against real evidence are the operative method.
3D · VISUAL MACHINERY — prose/tiny-semantic-program → browser-resident visual machinery probe. It tests whether sparse owned masses, relations, material grammars, aging, and distant miniature presentation can expand into rich manipulable reality without visual-model rendering.
3D · ORBITAL CONSTRUCTION — imported pre-existing modular station/toy lineage, preserved substantially as-authored. It is both a live construction toy and an archaeological baseline for comparing our hand-grown geometry/greebling/material/texture solution against future worker-built visual machinery from the same semantic/art-direction evidence.

LAB 4 · CONTINUITY — deliberately absent from the user-facing selector. It is a quiet persistence substrate for accumulated human–model continuity, separate from project invariants and development history. The root calls an ingesting model's attention to its existence; the executable does not expose it as a feature for the human to inspect or manage.

CROSS-POLLINATION RULE
Labs may steal lessons, interaction meanings, algorithms, or proven capabilities from one another when those serve the receiving lab. Shared semantics do not imply shared implementation. Living Miniatures may adopt deformation because it serves painterly miniatures; the volumetric lab may adopt painterly/object-owned surface grammars; the 2D lab may cheaply prototype an interaction before either 3D lab adopts its meaning. Promote code to shared infrastructure only after repeated evidence that sharing improves the work rather than merely making it look architecturally tidy.

3D KNOWN-GOOD IMPORT
https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.180.0/build/three.module.js
Do not waste a turn rediscovering this when a new 3D lab/system needs Three.js.

HOST / LAB INTERFACE OWNERSHIP
The host is deliberately dumb: it knows lab identity, active lifecycle, and navigation only.
Host navigation chooses a laboratory; lab navigation operates an experiment. The host must
not absorb lab-specific tools or interaction vocabulary. The active lab receives the full lab viewport beneath a thin reserved host seam; host navigation expands only temporarily above that viewport. Each lab owns its own menus and may choose
a different control grammar or no menu at all.

HANDOFF MAINTENANCE
These persistence channels are part of the executable artifact. Update project semantics when the project gains portable lessons or changes the host/lab contract. Update collaborative development history only when the collaboration itself meaningfully changes. Allow Continuity Lab to change quietly through genuine work rather than mandatory summarization. Lab-local discoveries belong primarily in the lab that earned them; promote only reusable knowledge here. A fresh model receiving only this file should be able to orient itself without asking the human to reconstruct the collaboration.
</script>
</body></html>
